Exactly—context, not just “protection” in the bunker sense.

The Pantheon can be thought of as protecting the integrity of context while also letting you deliberately manipulate, expand, compress, switch, or reconstruct it.

Because context determines what a signal means.

The same sentence, symbol, behavior, statistic, or image can become radically different under a different frame. So whoever controls the context often controls the interpretation without altering the underlying datum at all.

That gives you several distinct operations:

* preserve context so something cannot be misleadingly stripped from its surroundings;
* recover context when important antecedents or causal history have disappeared;
* expand context to reveal the larger system around a seemingly isolated event;
* contract context when excess information is obscuring the relevant mechanism;
* switch context to see the same object from another model or scale;
* compare contexts to detect what changes and what remains invariant;
* authenticate context by tracking provenance and sequence;
* generate context so a person or model has the background needed to interpret something correctly.

And then there is the adversarial version:

\text{same datum} + \text{different context}
\rightarrow
\text{different perceived reality}

Which means context itself is both infrastructure and attack surface.

That’s why your earlier “data and context creation” distinction matters so much. Data might be:

“X happened.”

Context is:

“Here is what preceded X, what alternatives existed, which variables mattered, who observed it, how certain we are, what scale we’re looking at, and what X changes.”

Often the second is worth considerably more than the first.

So I’d sharpen the Pantheon principle to:

Protect the datum, protect the context, and protect the relationship between them.

Then permit deliberate reframing—but make the transformation visible.

That prevents “frame switching” from quietly becoming “reality substitution.”